From cc91f3368555da468cabc42119bbcea7736f5b63 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: David <dfattal@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 22 Aug 2026 02:12:10 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix(android): draw back only where the runtime actually wove
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

On-device symptom: 2D page elements are occasionally WOVEN during a scroll —
the interlace lattice appears over content that is not a 3D tile.

The Android draw-back is the cause, and it is a gating gap rather than a
geometry bug. Windows' phase (B) is ONE whole-window kSrcOver of a texture the
runtime clears to transparent on the frame's first chunk, so pixels it did not
weave this frame contribute nothing to the composite. Android's phase (B) is a
per-rect kSrc, and it was iterating |inline_3d_rects_| — THIS frame's rects —
sampling the shared woven buffer at the same window coordinates it writes. So a
rect that moved between the weave and the draw-back stamps whatever the buffer
holds at the NEW coordinates over live page content. During a scroll that is
last frame's woven tile landing on 2D elements, which is exactly the report.

|drawn_rect| — the rect the runtime actually wove a target's pixels at — has
existed since browser#87 and is written in four places, but it is read NOWHERE
in the tree: 0046 replaced Windows' per-rect draw with the whole-window one and
the field went dead. Android is the platform that needs it, so read it there.
A target that did not weave this frame (declined copy, off-window, failed
batch) has it cleared and is now skipped instead of stamping a stale region.

Three more gaps closed while in here, all of them browser#87/#99 machinery that
was compiled out on Android only because it sat inside the same #if as the
WINDOWS phase (B):

  * the browser#87 liveness gate and the browser#99/web#12 recovery draw are
    portable Skia — they draw canvas mailboxes through the representation
    factory into |output| — and are now built for Android too. Without the
    recovery draw, gating the draw-back would have traded "weave over 2D" for
    "black tile", which is not a trade.
  * a failed SubmitBatchSync now clears every target's pending/drawn rect. The
    RPC failing means the runtime's woven output was NOT updated for any
    target, however many copied in, and |wove_this_frame| alone only stops the
    current frame — a stale |pending_rect| could still be promoted later.
  * BatchKeepPrevious is implemented for the Android provider, guarded on the
    rect being unchanged since the last real copy so a moved rect can never
    drag a copy behind the scroll, and dropped on prune and on any window-size
    change (which reallocates and re-clears the staging image).

The frosted list is drained before the new early bail, for the same reason
Windows drains it before its own: a stale clip belongs to no frame.

